    <statement><p>Use the Laws of logarithms to rewrite the expression
<me>\ln\left(\frac{x^{5}\sqrt{x-1}}{3x-9}\right)</me>
in a form with no logarithm of a product, quotient or power.</p>
<p>After rewriting we have
<me>\ln\left(\frac{x^{5}\sqrt{x-1}}{3x-9}\right)= A \ln x + B \ln (x-1)+ C \ln (3x-9)</me></p>
<p>with the constant
<m>A=</m> <fillin characters="25" name="AnSwEr0001"/></p>
<p>the constant
<m>B=</m> <fillin characters="25" name="AnSwEr0002"/></p>
<p>and the constant
<m>C=</m> <fillin characters="25" name="AnSwEr0003"/></p>
</statement>
    <answer>
      <p>
        <m>5</m>
      </p>
    </answer>
    <answer>
      <p>
        <m>0.5</m>
      </p>
    </answer>
    <answer>
      <p>
        <m>-1</m>
      </p>
    </answer>
